From a2c56e3abf17e86a3f862e7f68eeda42becc09df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: oslab2 <oslab2@oslab2>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 09:54:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] pre-gso: step 3 - page-frag staging, exact memory
 accounting, TCP_REPAIR

Accumulator (paper Fig. 8 Message Accumulator) now stores data the same
way tcp_sendmsg_locked() does, instead of one growing linear skb:

- Headless staging skb; bytes copied into sk_page_frag() pages with
  skb_copy_to_page_nocache(). Removes pskb_expand_head() re-copies,
  per-batch high-order (order-3) allocations, and the extra copy that
  skb_segment() made of linear data at software GSO.
- Socket memory charged byte-for-byte by the standard helpers
  (sk_wmem_schedule per chunk, then wmem_queued/forward_alloc charge),
  fixing the schedule(growth) vs charge(truesize delta) mismatch.
- Messages are accepted atomically: on -EFAULT, frag budget exhaustion
  or memory shortage the skb is rolled back (frags, page refs, lengths,
  charge) and the iov_iter rewound, so one send() is still exactly one
  message and the Virtual Split return value stays exact.
- Never wait for socket memory while holding staged bytes (they are
  charged but not in flight): commit first, then wait.
- Frag budget exhausted -> commit and retry (commit_grow_fail).
- TCP_REPAIR on: commit staged bytes first so checkpoint tools that dump
  the write queue (CRIU) do not lose them.
- Empty (rolled-back) staging skbs are not counted as abandoned batches.
- debugfs: skb_expand_* counters removed (path no longer exists).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
